## Abstract

**Background:** Hospital readmission within 30 days of inpatient discharge is a primary metric of healthcare quality, patient safety, and resource utilization across health systems. Developing reliable, automated risk prediction models requires standardized Electronic Health Record (EHR) data pipelines that function consistently across distinct clinical environments without relying on site-specific coding practices.

**Objective:** To establish a standardized multi-center benchmark comparing {{ models.n | words }} traditional machine learning architectures—{{ arch_long | join_and }}—for predicting 30-day all-cause hospital readmission across {{ nsites_word }} major academic health systems within the {{ consortium }} ({{ consortium_short }}) research network.

**Methods:** We conducted a multi-site retrospective cohort study of {{ cohort.n | n }} adult patients with diabetes, each represented by one qualifying inpatient stay drawn at random from that patient's eligible stays, across {{ nsites_word }} academic health systems within the {{ consortium }} ({{ consortium_short }}) research network (anonymized as {{ site_span }}, in compliance with multi-institution data governance policies). Each system's PCORnet extract was loaded into an OMOP CDM v5.4 database and the cohort was defined with the open-source omop-duck-db package: adults with an inpatient or emergency-to-inpatient stay of at least one day, at least 365 days of prior observation, a diabetes diagnosis, antidiabetic medication or HbA1c ≥ 6.5%, discharge alive, and a verified 30-day follow-up window. Predictors were vital signs and laboratory values (mean of the measurements within the index admission), prior-year healthcare utilization, 1-year medication exposure, 31 Elixhauser comorbidity domains from diagnoses recorded up to discharge, demographics, and address-derived Social Deprivation Index (SDI) and rurality (RUCA). Index admissions ran from {{ window.start_text }} to {{ window.end_text }}. All models were trained on 50/50 balanced training folds and evaluated on out-of-fold predictions from 5-fold cross-validation, scored on the natural distribution, with bootstrap confidence intervals. Hyperparameters were tuned targeting the F1 score across a predefined grid evaluated under 5-fold cross-validation, with configurations selected by F1 and by AUC-ROC compared with fixed baselines.{% if sensitivity %} Sensitivity analyses repeated the draw with {{ sensitivity.n_seeds_word }} random seeds and used all eligible stays.{% endif %}

**Results:** The cohort comprised {{ cohort.n | n }} adult patients with diabetes, each represented by one qualifying stay drawn at random from that patient's eligible stays ({{ cohort.n_readmitted | n }} followed by a 30-day readmission; {{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }}); index admissions strictly ran from {{ window.start_month }} to {{ window.end_month }}. {% if models.best in models.tree_models and models.boosting_beats_lr %}Gradient boosting architectures achieved the highest discrimination{% else %}{{ models.best }} achieved the highest discrimination{% endif %} ({{ ref.performance }}).

Out-of-fold AUC-ROC (95% bootstrap CI) with 50/50 balanced training: {% for name, m in M.items() %}{{ name }} {{ m.auc_text }}{{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %} At the 0.50 threshold, sensitivity was {{ models.sens_range[0] | fmt }} to {{ models.sens_range[1] | fmt }} and specificity {{ models.spec_range[0] | fmt }} to {{ models.spec_range[1] | fmt }}.

Hyperparameter tuning confirmed that selecting configurations by AUC-ROC yields stable discrimination, whereas selecting by F1 alters threshold operating trade-offs without improving ranking{% if ref.grid %} ({{ ref.grid }}){% endif %}.

