**Conclusions:** Standardized multi-domain EHR features derived with open OMOP tooling gave modest discrimination for 30-day readmission at a randomly selected qualifying admission (AUC-ROC {{ models.auc_range[0] | fmt }} to {{ models.auc_range[1] | fmt }} across architectures); the cohort definition is reproducible with open software.{% if stay_type %} The randomly drawn stay is a mix of first and later admissions, which differ in baseline risk and in the prior history available, so discrimination depends on which admission is scored; it was {% if stay_type.first.auc < models.list[stay_type.model].auc and stay_type.later.auc < models.list[stay_type.model].auc %}lower within each stay type than in the pooled draw{% else %}different within each stay type than in the pooled draw{% endif %}.{% endif %} Tuning the hyperparameters changed discrimination little; selecting them by F1 at a fixed threshold mainly moved the sensitivity and specificity trade-off, so AUC-ROC was the more stable selection criterion.

## 1 Introduction

30-day all-cause hospital readmissions pose a substantial burden on healthcare systems, reflecting ongoing patient vulnerability, sub-optimal post-discharge transitions, and high financial costs under value-based care initiatives such as the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) Hospital Readmissions Reduction Program (HRRP). Proactive risk stratification models embedded directly within EHR clinical workflows can assist care management teams in prioritizing post-discharge follow-up, medication reconciliation, and home health services.

Building risk prediction models that generalize across multiple health systems presents significant methodological challenges. Disparities in electronic documentation practices, localized laboratory ordering behaviors, variations in billing workflows, and differences in regional patient demographics often degrade model performance when algorithms trained on one institution are applied to another.

To address these challenges, the {{ consortium }} ({{ consortium_short }}) consortium established a multi-institutional research infrastructure to standardize longitudinal EHR data across diverse health systems. In this benchmark study, we evaluate {{ models.n | words }} established traditional machine learning algorithms across {{ cohort.n | n }} unique patients, each represented by one randomly drawn qualifying inpatient stay, from {{ nsites_word }} distinct clinical sites, designated {{ site_span }} to preserve institutional confidentiality in accordance with consortium data use and publication guidelines.

We describe the comprehensive epidemiological cohort derivation, feature engineering, laboratory consolidation from OMOP vocabulary hierarchies, feature scaling, temporal window alignment, and comparative model evaluation. This manuscript provides a descriptive methods and results baseline for the consortium. Standalone table deliverables are also maintained as standalone Excel ({{ files.workbook }}) and CSV files for direct editing.

## 2 Methods

### 2.1 Cohort Derivation & Eligibility Criteria

The {{ nsites_word }} health systems' PCORnet extracts of adults with diabetes were loaded into OMOP CDM v5.4 databases with omop-duck-db. The study cohort was then defined inside OMOP with the package's define_study_cohort() function, with no externally supplied list of index stays (the exact call is given in the Data & Code Availability section). The index stays in the analytic cohort were strictly restricted to admissions between {{ window.start_text }} and {{ window.end_text }}{% if window.last_discharge_text %} (last discharge {{ window.last_discharge_text }}){% endif %}, enforcing 100% fidelity to the grant protocol and DUA parameters.{% if site_dates %} Index stays spanned all {{ nsites_word }} participating health systems ({% for s in site_dates %}{{ s.label }}, {{ s.first }} to {{ s.last }}{{ "; " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %}).{% endif %} Criteria were applied in this order:

1. Index stay: inpatient (OMOP visit concept 9201) or emergency-room-to-inpatient (262) stays. Observation and emergency-department-only visits are used only to count prior utilization.
2. Age: at least 18 years at admission.
3. Length of stay: at least 1 day.
4. Wash-in: at least 365 days of observation before admission.
5. Discharge alive: stays ending in in-hospital death were excluded (Section 2.1.1).
6. Diabetes phenotype: an ICD-9 249/250 or ICD-10 E08–E13 diagnosis, an antidiabetic medication, or HbA1c ≥ 6.5% on or before the index date; patients whose only diabetes-related medication exposure was metformin and who had prediabetes or polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) were excluded.
7. Verified 30-day follow-up: a readmission within 30 days of discharge, or any recorded encounter on or after day 30 after discharge (Section 2.1.1).
8. One randomly drawn stay per patient (primary analysis): every stay that met the criteria above is a candidate index stay, with its own 30-day outcome and its own history window. For each patient one candidate was drawn uniformly at random (a seeded pseudo-random draw, so the selection is reproducible), so every patient is one observation and the cohort has no repeated patients.{% if sensitivity %} Sensitivity analyses (i) repeat the draw with {{ sensitivity.n_further_seeds_word }} further seeds and (ii) keep every candidate stay ({{ attrition.qualifying_stays | n }} stays; patient-grouped cross-validation).{% endif %}


Predictors were taken from the index stay and from the history before it; none uses information recorded after discharge. Vital signs and laboratory values are means of the measurements recorded within the index admission (admission to discharge); utilization counts cover the 365 days before admission; medication exposure covers the 365 days before discharge; comorbidity flags use all diagnoses recorded on or before the discharge date; and the 30-day outcome window starts on the day after discharge.

#### 2.1.1 Exclusion Criteria & Temporal Alignment

1. In-Hospital Mortality & Acute Care Facility Transfers: Stays ending in in-hospital death (a discharge-to-deceased concept or a death record on or before discharge) were excluded because patients were not at risk for post-discharge readmission. Stays discharged to another acute-care hospital (PCORnet discharge status IP, OMOP concept 8717) were also excluded, because the patient's care continued outside the capturing network.
2. Discharges Against Medical Advice (AMA) Retained: Patients discharged against medical advice (AMA) were explicitly retained as an eligible discharge status. While traditional administrative quality penalties (such as CMS HRRP) sometimes exclude AMA encounters, in prospective clinical practice AMA patients represent a clinically important high-risk subgroup who exhibit markedly elevated readmission rates ({{ cohort.ama_rate_pct | pct(1) }} vs. {{ cohort.non_ama_rate_pct | pct(1) }} for other discharges), with fragmented care transitions that clinical risk prediction models must actively identify.
3. Non-Target Screening Diagnoses: Patients with a prediabetes or polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) diagnosis whose only diabetes-related medication exposure was metformin were excluded, because metformin use alone does not establish diabetes in these conditions.
4. Incomplete Follow-Up Window Exclusion: Patients without a verified 30-day follow-up window (no readmission within 30 days and no recorded encounter on or after day 30) were excluded (stays with unverified follow-up status were excluded), while patients whose post-discharge encounter within 30 days was an acute readmission are included in the readmitted outcome group (Y = 1).
5. Strict Disjoint Temporal Scoping: Predictors come from the index stay and the history before it, and the outcome is any inpatient or emergency-to-inpatient stay that starts on days 1 to 30 after discharge, so predictors and outcome never overlap.

After applying these criteria, {{ cohort.n | n }} unique patients across the {{ nsites_word }} anonymized sites had at least one qualifying stay{% if attrition %} ({{ attrition.qualifying_stays | n }} qualifying stays in all, {{ attrition.stays_per_patient | fmt(2) }} per patient on average){% endif %}. The primary cohort has one randomly drawn stay per patient ({{ cohort.n | n }} stays), with a 30-day readmission rate of {{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }} (N = {{ cohort.n_readmitted | n }} followed by a readmission; N = {{ cohort.n_not | n }} not).{% if sensitivity and sensitivity.all_stays_rate_pct %} The rate across all {{ attrition.qualifying_stays | n }} qualifying stays is {{ sensitivity.all_stays_rate_pct | pct(1) }}, higher because patients who are admitted repeatedly contribute more stays.{% endif %}

