### 2.5 Model Evaluation Protocol & Classification Thresholding

Models were evaluated using 5-fold stratified cross-validation on Out-of-Fold (OOF) predictions, with folds grouped by patient (scikit-learn StratifiedGroupKFold, shuffled, seed {{ run.random_state if run.random_state is not none else 42 }}) so that no patient's stays appear in both training and test folds. Within each training fold, readmitted stays were resampled with replacement until the two classes were equal in number (50/50 balanced training); test folds were left at their natural prevalence, so every reported metric describes performance on the real-world distribution. Confidence intervals come from {% if run.bootstraps %}{{ run.bootstraps }} {% endif %}bootstrap replicates of the out-of-fold predictions (patients resampled with replacement). Given the {{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }} stay-level readmission base rate, predicted continuous probabilities p ∈ [0, 1] were evaluated using Brier scores; because balanced training imposes a 50% prior, these probabilities are not calibrated to the natural readmission rate. Classification metrics (Sensitivity, Specificity, F1) are reported at a default 0.50 decision threshold; in clinical deployment, thresholds are customizable to match institutional care management intervention capacity (e.g., selecting top 10% or top 20% highest-risk patients). Performance was quantified using key metrics:

1. AUC-ROC: Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve.
2. F1 Score: Harmonic mean of precision and recall at the 0.50 decision threshold.
3. Sensitivity (Recall): Proportion of true readmissions correctly identified.
4. Specificity: Proportion of non-readmissions correctly identified.
5. Brier Score: Mean squared difference between predicted probability and actual outcome (calibration measure; range 0–1, lower is better).

Model explainability was evaluated using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) summary plots (Lundberg and Lee 2017) and feature dependence analysis to visualize non-linear risk relationships.

