{% set ex = lr.example %}Note on Odds Ratio Formats & Column Definitions: 1. Adjusted Odds Ratio (95% CI): The risk multiplier per the increment in the Increment column{% if decisions.table6_increments != "sd" %} (e.g., +10 mmHg systolic pressure, +10 mg/dL BUN, +5 mEq/L sodium){% endif %}, or presence versus absence for indicators, with all other model terms held constant{% if ex %} ({{ ex.feature }}: OR {{ ex.or | fmt(2) }} (95% CI {{ ex.lo | fmt(2) }}–{{ ex.hi | fmt(2) }}){% if ex.increment %} per {{ ex.increment }}{% endif %}){% endif %}. 2. Standardized Beta: The log-odds change per one standard deviation of the feature (indicators included), which allows ranking of relative predictor strength across disparate clinical measurement domains. Because it scales with the feature's spread, it is not comparable with the odds ratio per increment.{% if lr.protective %} 3. Interpreting protective associations: Several terms show odds ratios {% if lr.protective | map(attribute="or") | min >= 0.8 %}slightly {% endif %}below 1 with p < 0.05 ({{ lr.protective_names | map("lower_first") | join_and }}). These are conditional associations among stays that were already admitted, adjusted for prior utilization, length of stay and other comorbidities, and they may reflect the mechanisms below rather than any effect of the factor itself. They should not be read as causal protection: conditioning on an index admission (collider stratification), correlated comorbidity indicators, and treatment and outpatient-management patterns that distinguish stable patients can all reverse the sign seen in unadjusted comparisons.{% endif %} {% if lr.protective %}4{% else %}3{% endif %}. {% if decisions.table6_missing == "indicator" %}A laboratory value or vital sign that was not measured for at least 11 stays has its own "not measured" term, so its odds ratio describes the stays where it was measured.{% else %}Continuous laboratory values were median-imputed (see Preprocessing), so their coefficients describe the effect among imputed and measured values together.{% endif %} Odds ratios are rounded to two decimals, so an odds ratio shown as 1.00 with a small p-value is a small per-increment effect, not a null one.


