## 4 Discussion & Conclusions

This multi-center study establishes a standardized benchmark for 30-day hospital readmission risk prediction across {{ cohort.n | n }} patients, each scored at one randomly drawn qualifying stay, at {{ nsites_word }} major academic health systems. By restricting predictors to the index stay and the history before it and constructing strictly disjoint post-discharge outcome windows [t~discharge~, t~discharge~ + 30 days] (Section 2.1.1), all evaluated model architectures—whether linear baselines (Logistic Regression) or traditional machine learning ensembles{% if models.tree_models %} ({{ models.tree_models | join(', ') }}){% endif %}—predict risk under rigorous, prospectively aligned temporal boundaries.

Under this multi-center evaluation protocol (N = {{ cohort.n | n }}), {% if models.boosting_beats_lr %}gradient boosting architectures (HistGBM and XGBoost AUC-ROC {{ models.boosting_auc_range[0] | fmt }}–{{ models.boosting_auc_range[1] | fmt }}) outperformed the linear Logistic Regression baseline ({{ M["Logistic Regression"].auc | fmt }}){% else %}the architectures reached AUC-ROC {{ models.auc_range[0] | fmt }}–{{ models.auc_range[1] | fmt }}, with no consistent advantage of gradient boosting over Logistic Regression ({{ M["Logistic Regression"].auc | fmt }}){% endif %}. With 50/50 balanced training, sensitivity at a 0.50 threshold ranged from {{ models.sens_range[0] | fmt }} to {{ models.sens_range[1] | fmt }} with specificity of {{ models.spec_range[0] | fmt }} to {{ models.spec_range[1] | fmt }}.

### 4.1 Comparison to Established Clinical Risk Rules

Traditional bedside risk scores—such as the LACE Index (Length of stay, Acuity, Comorbidities, Emergency visits) and the HOSPITAL Score—are widely utilized in hospital workflows due to manual scoring simplicity. In large-scale EHR validation literature, LACE typically achieves moderate discriminatory accuracy (AUC-ROC ≈ 0.65–0.68).

The choice of stay matters for interpreting the headline figure. The primary analysis draws one eligible stay per patient at random, so every patient counts once and its AUC-ROC ({{ models.auc_range[0] | fmt }} to {{ models.auc_range[1] | fmt }}) describes prediction at a randomly chosen admission, mixing first and later admissions that differ in baseline risk and prior history.{% if sensitivity %} Scoring every eligible stay (weighting frequently admitted patients by their number of stays) gave {{ sensitivity.all_stays_auc_text }}; the random draw was stable across seeds.{% endif %} Readers comparing these results with other studies should check which stay was scored and whether patients or stays were weighted equally. The readmission rate ({{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }}) is a per-patient rate at the drawn stay{% if sensitivity %}; it is lower than the rate across all stays ({{ sensitivity.all_stays_rate_pct | pct(1) }}){% endif %} and should not be compared with program benchmarks without matching their definitions of eligible and planned readmissions.

### 4.2 Limitations & Selection Bias Considerations

Ascertainment of the 30-day readmission outcome required a verified post-discharge follow-up signal (a readmission within 30 days, or any later documented encounter confirming continued observation within the health system network). Patients whose only available signal after their index discharge was silence — no further encounter of any kind within the network — were excluded as unverifiable rather than assumed non-readmitted (stays with unverified follow-up status were excluded). This protocol protects against misclassifying patients who were readmitted to outside health systems as false-negative non-readmissions; however, it introduces a potential selection bias by excluding patients who died or permanently relocated outside the regional health network shortly after discharge. Quantifying the direction and magnitude of this selection bias through linkage with national death registries (e.g. Social Security Death Master File) or regional Health Information Exchanges (HIEs) represents an important area for future study.

The primary cohort draws one stay per patient at random, so it discards the other eligible stays of patients with several admissions{% if attrition %} ({{ attrition.qualifying_stays | n }} eligible stays in all){% endif %}, and the readmission rate and AUC-ROC depend on which admission is scored. Index admissions were strictly restricted to the {{ window.start }} to {{ window.end }} protocol period (spanning from {{ window.start_text }} to {{ window.end_text }}), standardizing the observation window across all {{ nsites_word }} systems. Primary models were tuned targeting the F1 score across a predefined grid; while hyperparameter tuning produced small gains in rank discrimination over fixed configurations{% if ref.grid %} ({{ ref.grid }}){% endif %}, it achieved balanced sensitivity and specificity at the operational threshold.{% if completeness.sdi_missing_pct is not none and completeness.sdi_missing_pct %} Address-based SDI and RUCA are missing for about {{ completeness.sdi_missing_pct | round | int }}% of stays overall.{% endif %} Predictors use diagnoses recorded up to the discharge date, which are often finalized after discharge, so the models would not have all of these inputs at the bedside.

