## Appendix A: Study Resampling & Cross-Validation Methodology

### Study Resampling and Cross-Validation Framework

Model training and validation were conducted using a rigorous 5-fold stratified, patient-grouped cross-validation scheme designed to evaluate generalizability, prevent data leakage, and ensure reproducible risk estimation across the {{ nsites_word }} health systems.

### Stratified, Patient-Grouped 5-Fold Partitioning

To keep each patient entirely in training or entirely in validation and maintain similar class balance across subsets, the primary cohort (N = {{ cohort.n | n }} patients, one randomly drawn stay each) was partitioned into five mutually exclusive folds, grouping on the patient and stratifying on the 30-day readmission outcome (Y ∈ {0, 1}). Each fold approximately preserves the readmission rate of {{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }} (N = {{ cohort.n_readmitted | n }} events); no patient appears in both a training and a test fold.

In each fold iteration: 1. Four folds (about 80% of patients and their stays) were assigned to model fitting and parameter estimation. 2. One held-out fold (about 20% of patients and their stays) served as the strictly unseen evaluation set. 3. Out-of-fold (OOF) predicted probabilities p̂~i~ ∈ [0, 1] were aggregated across all five evaluation folds to compute consortium-wide performance metrics without optimistic bias.

### Preprocessing and Imputation Isolation

To prevent information leakage from test folds into training steps: Missing Value Imputation: Continuous laboratory measures and vital signs were imputed using median values computed exclusively from the active training partition (4 folds) and subsequently applied to the corresponding held-out evaluation fold. Feature Normalization: Z-score standardization (used for Logistic Regression only) was fit on training folds only; the tree-based models use unscaled features.

### 50/50 Balanced Training Protocol

Given the stay-level base rate ({{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }}), standard machine learning loss functions naturally optimize for overall classification accuracy by favoring the majority non-readmitted class, yielding high specificity at the expense of sensitivity. All models in this study were therefore trained on balanced data: 1. The training partition (80% of data) was resampled by drawing readmitted stays with replacement until they matched the number of non-readmitted stays (a 1:1 class ratio, 50% readmitted, 50% non-readmitted). 2. Model parameters were estimated on this balanced training distribution. 3. Evaluation on the Real-World Prospective Distribution: Trained models were evaluated exclusively on the unadjusted, natural-prevalence held-out test fold ({{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }} readmission rate) at a standard decision threshold of τ = 0.50. This protocol recovers clinically useful sensitivity ({{ ref.performance }}) while measuring true prospective discriminatory power.

### Non-Parametric Bootstrap Confidence Intervals

Statistical uncertainty for all evaluation metrics (AUROC, AUPRC, Sensitivity, Specificity, F1-Score, and Brier Score) was quantified using non-parametric bootstrap replicates of the out-of-fold predictions: {{ run.bootstraps }} replicates in which patients were resampled with replacement. Two-sided 95% confidence intervals were computed using the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the empirical bootstrap distribution.

