### Biostatistical Evaluation Metrics

Model performance for predicting 30-day binary readmission was assessed using standardized discriminatory, classification, and calibration metrics:

#### Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC)

Measures threshold-independent discriminatory ability across all possible operating cutoffs:

$$\text{AUROC} = \int_0^1 \text{Sensitivity}\left(\text{FPR}^{-1}(t)\right) dt$$

An AUROC of 0.50 denotes chance discrimination, whereas 1.0 indicates perfect class separation.

#### Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC)

Evaluates precision across varying recall thresholds. Particularly informative in imbalanced binary classification where true readmissions represent a minority subset ({{ cohort.rate_pct | pct }}):

$$\text{AUPRC} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} (\text{Recall}_k - \text{Recall}_{k-1}) \cdot \text{Precision}_k$$

#### Brier Score (Probability Calibration)

Quantifies mean squared error between predicted readmission probabilities p̂~i~ ∈ [0, 1] and actual binary outcomes y~i~ ∈ {0, 1}:

$$\text{Brier} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (\hat{p}_i - y_i)^2$$

Scores range from 0 (perfect calibration and accuracy) to 1.{% if cohort.rate_pct %} For an uninformative model assigning the base prevalence (ȳ ≈ {{ (cohort.rate_pct / 100) | fmt }} in the primary randomly drawn cohort) to every patient, the baseline Brier score is ȳ(1 − ȳ) ≈ {{ (cohort.rate_pct / 100 * (1 - cohort.rate_pct / 100)) | fmt }}. Under 50/50 balanced training evaluated on the natural-prevalence test fold, Brier scores range from {{ models.brier_range[0] | fmt }}–{{ models.brier_range[1] | fmt }}; they exceed the uninformative baseline because balanced training shifts predicted probabilities toward a 50% prior, not because the models rank patients poorly.{% endif %}{% if sensitivity and sensitivity.all_stays_prevalence %} In the all-eligible-stays sensitivity analysis (ȳ ≈ {{ sensitivity.all_stays_prevalence | fmt }}, baseline Brier ≈ {{ (sensitivity.all_stays_prevalence * (1 - sensitivity.all_stays_prevalence)) | fmt }}) the balanced-training scores are {{ sensitivity.all_stays_brier_text }}.{% endif %}

#### Classification Metrics at Decision Threshold τ = 0.50

- Sensitivity (True Positive Rate / Recall): $\frac{TP}{TP + FN}$
- Specificity (True Negative Rate): $\frac{TN}{TN + FP}$
- F1-Score: Harmonic mean of Precision and Recall: $\frac{2 \cdot TP}{2 \cdot TP + FP + FN}$

### Model Explainability (TreeSHAP)

To explain model predictions, Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) based on cooperative game theory (Lundberg and Lee 2017) were calculated using the TreeSHAP algorithm. For each patient encounter x, the model prediction is decomposed additively into feature contributions:

$$f(x) = \phi_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{M} \phi_j(x)$$

where ϕ~0~ is the base expected model prediction and ϕ~j~(x) represents the marginal impact of feature j on readmission risk.
